From ccd5202e75be0f969ed412dd2e019c30e594f31e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Liberty <mliberty@precisioninno.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 02:08:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/13] python: per-tool modules sharing one extension library

The Bazel Python bindings compiled every tool into one combined module,
openroadpy, with odb, utl and rmp as separate extensions. The combined
module flattened all tools into one namespace (cts and wmk both define
CtsOptions, so wmk's was renamed), and each separate extension carried its
own static copy of odb, utl and the logger.

Link every tool's SWIG module (ord_py, odb, utl, ant_py, cts_py, ...) into
one library, _openroad.so. python/modules/_openroad_loader.py loads each
module's init function from that library with ExtensionFileLoader, the
counterpart of CMake's PyImport_AppendInittab: the tools keep separate
namespaces while the dynamic loader maps the library once, so they share
one copy of the C++ state. `import openroad` imports every tool module, as
`openroad -python` does at startup, so objects Design returns get their
tool's class. The flat openroadpy module is gone; its one user,
test/downstream, now imports from openroad.

- The wheel ships _openroad.so instead of _openroadpy.so, _odb.so, _utl.so
  and _rmp_py.so (52 MB, from 64 MB).
- odb_py, utl_py and rmp_py are aliases of //:openroad_py.
- par's vector templates were skipped under Bazel to avoid duplicates in
  the combined module; as its own module par needs them.
- ord::getLogger() falls back to the default logger when no Tech exists,
  so utl's Python logging works before a Tech is created and otherwise
  uses the Tech's logger.
- test/helpers.py takes PlaceOptions from gpl under Bazel too.
- modules_test checks the separate namespaces, the single library mapping,
  objects crossing modules and the shared database.

Signed-off-by: Matt Liberty <mliberty@precisioninno.com>
